# Line endings are pinned to LF.
#
# Git's autocrlf behaviour differs per machine, and a repository that is written
# on Windows and built on Linux will otherwise produce files that fail on Linux:
# a bash script committed with CRLF endings is rejected by the interpreter with
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M", and the scheduled workflow dies before it
# runs a scan. Pinning the endings in the repository is the only way to make the
# checked-in content authoritative on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Sources and configuration that must stay LF.
*.go   text eol=lf
*.mod  text eol=lf
*.sum  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Captured pages are fixtures and are stored byte-for-byte.
#
# They are evidence: assertions are pinned to these exact bytes, and the parser
# locates its payload by matching a marker inside the document. Converting line
# endings would silently change the thing the tests are verifying, so these files
# are excluded from any transformation.
fixtures/** -text

# Build output is never tracked; this is belt-and-braces with .gitignore.
*.exe -text
